<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Find the Greatest Common Factor: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01547</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Find the Greatest Common Factor: Mini-Lesson Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ffceb9a7-35a4-4bf6-80eb-d768598e35d5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <note class="mini-lesson-question" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Question #3: Find the Greatest Common Factor</title>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00001"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_OR_U6_Q3"/></para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
    </note>


    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Find the Greatest Common Factor</title>
      <para id="para-00002">The greatest common factor (GCF) of two or more expressions is the
        largest expression that is a factor of all the expressions.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">Find the greatest common factor of <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>54</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>36</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00004"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Factor each number into primes.</para>
      <para id="para-00005"> Factor <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>54</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>36</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00006">
        <media alt="A factor tree for 54: 54 splits into 9 and 6. 9 splits into 3 and 3. 6 splits into 2 and 3. The prime factors 3, 3, 3, and 2 are highlighted in boxes.">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_08_01.jpg" width="100"/>
        </media>

      </para>
      <para id="para-00007">
        <media alt="A factor tree for 36. 35 splits into 6 and 6. 6 splits into 2 and 3. 6 splits into 2 and 3. The prime factors 3, 2, 3, and 2 are highlighted in boxes.">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_08_02.jpg" width="100"/>
        </media>

      </para>
      <para id="para-00008"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - List the factors. In each column, circle
        the common factors.</para>
      <para id="para-00009"> Circle the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>, <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>, and <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> that are shared by both numbers.</para>
      <para id="para-00010">
        <media alt="Factorizations of 36 and 54 are shown, with 36 = 2 times 2 times 3 times 3 and 54 = 2 times 3 times 3 times 3; matching factors are circled to highlight common factors between the two numbers.">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_08_03.jpg" width="157"/>
        </media>

      </para>
      <para id="para-00011"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Bring down the common factors that all the
        expressions share.</para>
      <para id="para-00012"> Bring down the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>, <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>, and <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00013"> GCF <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            <m:mi>·</m:mi>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            <m:mi>·</m:mi>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00014"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Multiply the factors.</para>
      <para id="para-00015"> Multiply the common factors <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>, <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>, and <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00016"> GCF <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>18</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00017"> The GCF of <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>54</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>36</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> is <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>18</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>
    </section>
    <note class="try" id="note-00001">
      <title>Find the Greatest Common Factor</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00018">Find the greatest common factor (GCF) of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>84</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>56</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00019"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00020">Here’s how to find the GCF.</para>
          <para id="para-00021"><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis>- Factor each number into primes.</para>
          <para id="para-00022"> Factor <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>84</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>56</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">
            <media alt="A factor tree for 84: 84 splits into 14 and 6. 14 splits into 2 and 7. 6 splits into 2 and 3. The prime factors 2, 7, 2, and 3 are highlighted in boxes.">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_08_04.jpg" width="100"/>
            </media>

          </para>
          <para id="para-00024">
            <media alt="A factor tree for 56: 56 splits into 4 and 14. 4 splits into 2 and 2. 14 splits into 2 and 7. The prime factors 2, 2, 2, and 7 are highlighted in boxes.">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_08_05.jpg" width="100"/>
            </media>

          </para>
          <para id="para-00025"><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis> List the factors. In each column,
      circle the common factors.</para>
          <para id="para-00026"> Circle the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>7</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> that are shared by both
      numbers.</para>
          <para id="para-00027">
            <media alt="Factorizations of 84 and 56 are shown, with 84 = 2 times 2 times 3 times 7 and 56 = 2 times 2 times 2 times 7; matching factors are circled to highlight common factors between the two numbers.">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_08_06.jpg" width="205"/>
            </media>

          </para>
          <para id="para-00028"><emphasis>Step 3 </emphasis>- Bring down the common factors that all
      the expressions share.</para>
          <para id="para-00029"> Bring down the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>7</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00030">GCF <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>227</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00031"><emphasis>Step 4 </emphasis>- Multiply the factors.</para>
          <para id="para-00032"> Multiply the common factors <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>7</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00033"> GCF <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>28</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00034"> The GCF of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>84</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>56</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>28</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </note>
    <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003">
      <title>Check Your Understanding</title>
      <exercise id="c513f2f5-07ca-44f3-8426-5c72e0ff79ee" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00035">Which is the greatest common factor of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>96</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>72</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?</para>
          <list list-type="enumerated" number-style="lower-alpha" id="list-00001">
            <item><para id="para-00037">
                <m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>24</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
              </para></item>
            <item><para id="para-00038">
                <m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>12</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
              </para></item>
            <item><para id="para-00039">
                <m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>36</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
              </para></item>
            <item><para id="para-00040">
                <m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>48</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
              </para></item>
          </list>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00041">24</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Videos: Greatest Common Factor of Two Integers</title>

      <para id="para-00043">Watch the following video to learn more about finding the greatest
        common factor of two integers.</para>
